; A bounded channel of words between `parallel` bindings (R-C2,
; docs/memory-model.md MM-PAR-10).
;
; WHAT IT IS. A ring of `cap` machine words in a MAP_SHARED mapping,
; with a lock and an event counter beside it. Any number of bindings may
; send and receive; `chanSend` blocks while the ring is full and
; `chanRecv` while it is empty; `chanClose` ends the stream, after which
; a send is refused and a receive drains what is left and then answers
; `None`. Values leave in the order they were sent (one total order over
; all senders, FIFO within each).
;
; WHY A MAPPING, AND WHY BOTH LOWERINGS. A binding runs in a forked child
; by default and in a thread under `--threads`. An arena page is private
; after a fork - a copy - so a ring allocated there would be two rings.
; A mapping made with `sysMapShared` BEFORE the `parallel` form is the
; same pages in the parent and in every child, and in every thread, so
; one channel serves both lowerings without the program choosing.
;
; WHAT CROSSES IS A WORD. An `Int`. A heap value cannot: a forked child
; has its own arena, and a thread's arena is unmapped when the thread
; ends (MM-PAR-6a), so a handle sent through here would name memory the
; receiver does not own. Typed transfer of heap values is R-C2's open
; half.
;
; THE HANDLE IS A `Chan` (docs/memory-model.md MM-PAR-8): a word struct
; (MM-VAL-10a) that only this module can make or read, so no other code
; can hand a channel operation a word `chanNew` did not answer. Its word
; is a slot in the runtime's handle table, kind `chanKind`, naming the
; mapping, and every public function asks the table for the mapping
; first (`chanAt`). So an operation on a freed channel, and a second
; `chanFree`, trap with status 85 instead of touching the unmapped ring.
; It is declared `shared`, because every operation below is built for
; use from every binding at once, so a `parallel` binding may capture it
; in either lowering (AX3064 admits it). What stays the program's: free
; a channel only once no binding can still reach it - after the
; `parallel` form that used it. A free that RACES another binding's
; operation is a data race (MM-PAR-9): the check covers every operation
; ordered after the free, not one already in flight.
;
; HOW IT BLOCKS. Word 0 is the lock, and it NAMES ITS HOLDER, as
; `stdlib/Sync.ax`'s mutex word does: 0 when free, otherwise the holder's
; pid shifted left twice with bit 0 set when someone waits - Drepper's
; futex mutex, with "held" spelled as who holds it. The pid goes in with
; the compare-and-swap that takes the lock, so there is no instant at
; which the lock is held and the holder unknown. An uncontended acquire
; and release are one compare-and-swap each and no wait or wake; each
; public call asks `getpid` once for the mark (`chanMe`). Word 1 counts
; every change a waiter could be waiting for. A binding that must wait
; for the ring reads the counter under the lock, announces itself in
; word 2, releases, and sleeps in `sysWaitWordTimeout` while the counter
; still holds what it read - so a change made between its release and
; its sleep is seen on entry, and no wake is lost. A changer bumps the
; counter under the lock and wakes only if word 2 says someone waits.
; Where the target has no blocking wait (`waitWordKind` 0: FreeBSD for
; now) the same code spins: correct, and it costs a core.
;
; A HOLDER THAT DIES. `MM-PAR-7`'s sweep SIGKILLs a forked sibling
; wherever it is, so a binding can die between taking the lock and
; letting it go. Every wait here - for the lock or for the ring - sleeps
; in slices of at most `chanSliceNanos` (100 ms). A waiter whose slice
; runs out with the lock still held looks at the pid the word names:
; `kill(pid, 0)` answering ESRCH, or `waitid` saying that this
; process's own child of that pid has exited, means the holder died
; holding it. The waiter then POISONS the channel: one compare-and-swap
; takes the word from the dead holder's mark to `chanPoisonMark`, so it
; lands only while the word still names that holder, and every waiter
; is woken to see it. The lock is never handed over: a holder that dies
; published nothing, so its plain stores to the ring are in no order
; anyone can rely on. From then on the channel answers as closed and
; drained, without touching the ring: `chanSend` and `chanTrySend`
; answer `False`, `chanRecv` and `chanTryRecv` `None`, the timed forms
; `Err` with code `chanOwnerDead`, `chanClose` changes nothing,
; `chanClosed` answers `True`, `chanLen` 0, and `chanPoisoned` says
; why. `chanFree` takes no lock and frees it as usual. A binding
; already asleep - on the lock or on the ring - answers within one
; slice of the holder being found dead.
;
; TIMED FORMS (MM-PAR-12). `chanSendTimeout` and
; `chanRecvTimeout` wait at most a given number of nanoseconds and then
; answer `Err` with code `sysTimedOut`, having sent or taken nothing.
; The deadline covers the wait for the lock as well as the wait for the
; ring, so a holder that stays alive and never lets go - stopped, or
; dead but not yet reaped by a process other than this one - costs a
; timed call its deadline and no more. The wait is
; `sysWaitWordTimeout`, in slices; a slice the kernel timed out costs
; the whole slice and one cut short costs the clock's step, clamped to
; it, so a wait nobody ends is never shorter than asked. The ring is
; looked at once more after the last slice: a word that arrives as the
; time runs out is taken.
;
; WHAT IT DOES NOT PROVIDE. No timeout on `chanSend` and `chanRecv`
; themselves (a receive on a channel nobody closes waits forever, and a
; thread cannot be killed out of it - MM-PAR-7's sweep JOINS threads;
; the timed forms above are the way out of a wait nobody ends); no
; fairness between waiters (a wake wakes all, and whoever takes the lock
; first wins); no priority inheritance; not callable from a signal
; handler (the lock is not reentrant). A dead holder is found only once
; the kernel says it is gone: one that is dead but not yet reaped - a
; zombie its parent has not joined - looks alive to every binding but
; its parent, and a pid an unrelated process has taken makes a dead
; holder look alive for good. In both cases the untimed calls wait and
; the timed ones answer `sysTimedOut`.
; Words 3-7: head, tail, capacity, closed, mapping length.
(import Mem)
(import Sys)
(import Err)

; ---- the handle ------------------------------------------------------
; A channel: one word, a slot in the runtime's handle table (MM-PAR-8)
; naming the ring's mapping.
(pub struct Chan word shared
  (slot : Int))

; The handle table's kind for a channel. The table refuses a word of any
; other kind, so a mutex's handle is not a channel even to code that
; forged one.
(:: chanKind Int)
(fn (chanKind)
  2)

; The ring a live channel names, or the 85 trap for one that was freed.
(:: chanAt (-> Chan Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanAt c)
  (__handle_get chanKind c.slot))

; A handle for a fresh ring, or 0 when the table has no slot left.
(:: chanName (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanName ch)
  (__handle_new chanKind ch))

; Retire a live channel's handle and answer its ring, or the 85 trap for
; a channel already freed.
(:: chanRetire (-> Chan Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanRetire c)
  (__handle_free chanKind c.slot))

; ---- codes -------------------------------------------------------------
; What a timed call answers on a channel whose lock holder died: the
; mutex's `syncOwnerDead`, the same code for the same event. Above 255,
; like `sysTimedOut`, so it cannot be mistaken for a wait status.
(pub :: chanOwnerDead Int)
(pub fn (chanOwnerDead)
  1004)

; The lock word of a poisoned channel. Odd and below 4, so it is never
; 0 (free) and never a holder's mark (a pid times 4, plus 1).
(:: chanPoisonMark Int)
(fn (chanPoisonMark)
  3)

; ---- the lock --------------------------------------------------------
; This binding's mark in the lock word: its pid, shifted past bit 0.
; Asked once per public call and handed to every lock and unlock in it.
(:: chanMe Int)
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (chanMe)
  (* sysGetPid 4))

; Take the lock. Answers 0 once this binding holds it, 1 when the time
; in `b` ran out first (`timed` 1 only), and 2 when the channel is
; poisoned. An uncontended acquire is the one compare-and-swap.
(:: chanLock (-> Int Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanLock ch me b timed)
  (if (== (__atomic_cas ch 0 me) 0)
    0
    (chanAcquire ch me b timed)))

; The waiting loop behind `chanLock`: `Sync.ax`'s `syncAcquire`, with
; the poisoning a compare-and-swap of its own. A lock taken after a wait
; is marked contended, because another waiter may still sleep on it.
(:: chanAcquire (-> Int Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanAcquire ch me b timed)
  (let ((mut state 0))
    {
      (while (== state 0)
        (let ((v (__atomic_load ch)))
          (if (== v 0)
            (if (== (__atomic_cas ch 0 (+ me 1)) 0)
              (set state 1)
              0)
            (if (== v chanPoisonMark)
              (set state 2)
              (if (chanOutOfTime b timed)
                (set state 3)
                (let ((w (chanMarkWaiting ch v)))
                  (if (== w 0)
                    0
                    (let ((slice (chanLockSlice b timed)))
                      (let ((code (sysWaitWordTimeout ch w slice)))
                        {
                          (if (== timed 1)
                            (chanTimeSpent b code slice)
                            0)
                          (if (&& (== code 1) (chanHolderDead w me))
                            (chanPoison ch w)
                            0)
                        })))))))))
      (if (== state 1)
        0
        (if (== state 2)
          2
          1))
    }))

; Lock value `v` with the waiter bit set - already, or by this call - or
; 0 when the word moved first and the caller must look again.
(:: chanMarkWaiting (-> Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanMarkWaiting ch v)
  (if (== (% v 2) 1)
    v
    (if (== (__atomic_cas ch v (+ v 1)) v)
      (+ v 1)
      0)))

; Is the holder named by lock value `w` gone? Only a pid other than this
; binding's own can be (a thread's siblings share its pid, and are alive
; while it runs). ESRCH from `kill(pid, 0)` says so; so does `waitid`,
; for a child of THIS process that has exited and not been reaped - a
; zombie, which `kill` still answers for. Whether the word still names
; that holder is `chanPoison`'s compare-and-swap to decide.
(:: chanHolderDead (-> Int Int Bool))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (chanHolderDead w me)
  (let ((owner (/ w 4)))
    (if (|| (== owner 0) (== (* owner 4) me))
      false
      (match (sysKill owner 0)
        ((Ok r) (chanChildEnded owner))
        ((Err e) (== (errCode e) 3))))))

; Whether `pid` is this process's own child and has exited, unreaped.
(:: chanChildEnded (-> Int Bool))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanChildEnded pid)
  (let ((buf (chanBlock sysChildPollBytes)))
    (let ((ended (chanYes (sysChildExited pid buf))))
      {
        (chanBlockDone buf)
        ended
      })))

; `Ok True`, and nothing else, is yes: a look that failed has seen nothing.
(:: chanYes (-> (Result Bool Error) Bool))
(fn (chanYes r)
  (match r
    ((Ok b) b)
    ((Err e) false)))

; Take the word from the dead holder's mark `w` to the poison mark -
; only if it still holds `w` - and wake every binding asleep on the lock
; or on the ring, so each looks again and sees it.
(:: chanPoison (-> Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanPoison ch w)
  (if (== (__atomic_cas ch w chanPoisonMark) w)
    {
      (__atomic_add (+ ch 8) 1)
      (sysWakeWord ch)
      (sysWakeWord (+ ch 8))
      0
    }
    0))

; Whether a holder died holding this channel's lock.
(:: chanIsPoisoned (-> Int Bool))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanIsPoisoned ch)
  (== (__atomic_load ch) chanPoisonMark))

; Let the next holder in: `me` -> 0 is the uncontended release; a word
; with the waiter bit set is cleared and its waiters woken.
(:: chanUnlock (-> Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanUnlock ch me)
  (if (== (__atomic_cas ch me 0) me)
    0
    {
      (__atomic_store ch 0)
      (sysWakeWord ch)
      0
    }))

; ---- waiting for a change ---------------------------------------------
; Under the lock: announce a waiter and answer the counter it must wait
; on. The caller releases, calls `chanSleep`, and retries.
(:: chanPark (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanPark ch)
  {
    (__atomic_add (+ ch 16) 1)
    (__atomic_load (+ ch 8))
  })

; Sleep while the counter holds `seen`, for at most one slice: the caller
; takes the lock again either way, which is where a holder that died is
; noticed.
(:: chanSleep (-> Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanSleep ch seen)
  {
    (sysWaitWordTimeout (+ ch 8) seen chanSliceNanos)
    (__atomic_add (+ ch 16) (- 0 1))
    0
  })

; `chanSleep` for at most `nanos`: `sysWaitWordTimeout`'s answer - 1
; when the kernel measured the whole of it and nobody woke this waiter.
(:: chanSleepFor (-> Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanSleepFor ch seen nanos)
  (let ((code (sysWaitWordTimeout (+ ch 8) seen nanos)))
    {
      (__atomic_add (+ ch 16) (- 0 1))
      code
    }))

; ---- time left ---------------------------------------------------------
; A timed call keeps two words in a 32-byte scratch block: word 2 the
; nanoseconds it has left, word 3 the clock reading it last took (words
; 0-1 are the clock's own buffer). `chanTimeSpent` charges one wait to
; it: the whole slice when the kernel measured it (`code` 1), otherwise
; the clock's step, clamped to [0, slice] - so a step of Darwin's
; realtime clock (`sysTimeoutMicros`) moves the wait by at most the one
; slice it landed in, and a wait nobody wakes is never shorter than
; asked, on any target. `docs/memory-model.md` MM-PAR-12.
(:: chanScratch (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanScratch nanos)
  (let ((b (chanBlock 32)))
    {
      (__store64 b 2 nanos)
      (__store64 b 3 (sysTimeoutMicros b))
      b
    }))

; A block of `n` bytes with a share taken, and the share given back:
; the release files it for reuse (MM-LIFE-2k), so a loop that asks for
; one on every pass does not grow the arena.
(:: chanBlock (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanBlock n)
  (let ((b (memAlloc n)))
    {
      (__retain b)
      b
    }))

(:: chanBlockDone (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
;@axiom:precondition(`b` is a block `chanBlock` or `chanScratch` answered, handed back once and not used after)
(fn (chanBlockDone b)
  {
    (__release b)
    0
  })

(:: chanTimeLeft (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanTimeLeft b)
  (__load64 b 2))

(:: chanTimeSpent (-> Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanTimeSpent b code slice)
  (let (
    (left (__load64 b 2))
    (now (sysTimeoutMicros b))
  )
    (let ((step (chanStep code slice (* (- now (__load64 b 3)) 1000))))
      {
        (__store64 b 2 (- left step))
        (__store64 b 3 now)
        0
      })))

; How long the next wait may be: the time left, or `chanSliceNanos` if
; that is shorter. A slice bounds what one clock step can cost: with the
; whole remaining time as ONE slice, a woken wait charged a forward step
; of Darwin's realtime clock could end the entire wait early.
(:: chanSlice (-> Int Int))
(fn (chanSlice b)
  (if (< (chanTimeLeft b) chanSliceNanos)
    (chanTimeLeft b)
    chanSliceNanos))

; The same for a wait on the lock, which an untimed call makes too: it
; has no block, and no time that runs out.
(:: chanLockSlice (-> Int Int Int))
(fn (chanLockSlice b timed)
  (if (== timed 1)
    (chanSlice b)
    chanSliceNanos))

(:: chanOutOfTime (-> Int Int Bool))
(fn (chanOutOfTime b timed)
  (if (== timed 1)
    (<= (chanTimeLeft b) 0)
    false))

; The longest one wait sleeps before it looks again - at the ring, at the
; clock, and at a lock's holder: 100 ms, `Sync.ax`'s probe interval.
(:: chanSliceNanos Int)
(fn (chanSliceNanos)
  100000000)

; The nanoseconds one wait costs: all of `slice` when the kernel timed
; it out, otherwise the clock's step `d` clamped to [0, slice].
(:: chanStep (-> Int Int Int Int))
(fn (chanStep code slice d)
  (if (== code 1)
    slice
    (if (< d 0)
      0
      (if (> d slice)
        slice
        d))))

; Under the lock: a change a waiter may be waiting for.
(:: chanBump (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanBump ch)
  (__atomic_add (+ ch 8) 1))

; After the release: wake the waiters, if the word says there are any.
(:: chanNotify (-> Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanNotify ch)
  (if (> (__atomic_load (+ ch 16)) 0)
    (sysWakeWord (+ ch 8))
    0))

; ---- the words -------------------------------------------------------
; Word `i` of the channel, and a store to it. Plain accesses, made only
; under the lock, whose compare-and-swaps order them (MM-PAR-9). The two
; are the channel's only raw loads and stores of the ring; every public
; function below goes through them and the helpers above, which are
; trusted encapsulations, so no public row carries `Unsafe` but the
; timed forms' own (MM-PAR-10).
(:: chanGet (-> Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanGet ch i)
  (__load64 ch i))

(:: chanPut (-> Int Int Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanPut ch i v)
  (__store64 ch i v))

; ---- the channel ------------------------------------------------------
; A channel of `cap` words, 1 <= cap <= 1,048,576. Answers the handle,
; or the mapping's error; a capacity out of range is EINVAL (22 on every
; target with a syscall ABI), and a handle table with no slot left is
; EMFILE (24).
(pub :: chanNew (-> Int (Result Chan Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (chanNew cap)
  (if (|| (< cap 1) (> cap 1048576))
    (Err (mkError 22 "chanNew: capacity must be 1..1048576"))
    (let ((len (* 8 (+ cap 8))))
      (match (sysMapShared len)
        ((Ok ch)
          {
            (chanPut ch 5 cap)
            (chanPut ch 7 len)
            (chanNamed ch len)
          }
        )
        ((Err e) (Err e))))))

; Name a fresh ring in the handle table, or unmap it and say why not.
(:: chanNamed (-> Int Int (Result Chan Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(fn (chanNamed ch len)
  (let ((h (chanName ch)))
    (if (== h 0)
      (match (sysUnmapShared ch len)
        ((Ok z) (Err (mkError 24 "chanNew: every handle is in use")))
        ((Err e) (Err (mkError 24 "chanNew: every handle is in use"))))
      (Ok (Chan h)))))

; Send `v`, waiting while the ring is full. `True` once it is in the
; ring; `False` if the channel is closed - before the call or while it
; waited - or poisoned (`chanPoisoned`), and then `v` was not sent.
(pub :: chanSend (-> Chan Int Bool))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (chanSend c v)
  (let (
    (ch (chanAt c))
    (me chanMe)
    (mut state 0)
  )
    {
      (while (== state 0)
        (if (!= (chanLock ch me 0 0) 0)
          (set state 2)
          (let (
            (head (chanGet ch 3))
            (tail (chanGet ch 4))
            (cap (chanGet ch 5))
          )
            (if (== (chanGet ch 6) 1)
              {
                (chanUnlock ch me)
                (set state 2)
              }
              (if (< (- tail head) cap)
                {
                  (chanPut ch (+ 8 (% tail cap)) v)
                  (chanPut ch 4 (+ tail 1))
                  (chanBump ch)
                  (chanUnlock ch me)
                  (chanNotify ch)
                  (set state 1)
                }
                (let ((seen (chanPark ch)))
                  {
                    (chanUnlock ch me)
                    (chanSleep ch seen)
                  }))))))
      (== state 1)
    }))

; Receive the oldest word, waiting while the ring is empty and open.
; `None` once the channel is closed AND drained - the end of the stream
; - or poisoned (`chanPoisoned`).
(pub :: chanRecv (-> Chan (Option Int)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (chanRecv c)
  (let (
    (ch (chanAt c))
    (me chanMe)
    (mut state 0)
    (mut got 0)
  )
    {
      (while (== state 0)
        (if (!= (chanLock ch me 0 0) 0)
          (set state 2)
          (let (
            (head (chanGet ch 3))
            (tail (chanGet ch 4))
            (cap (chanGet ch 5))
          )
            (if (< head tail)
              {
                (set got (chanGet ch (+ 8 (% head cap))))
                (chanPut ch 3 (+ head 1))
                (chanBump ch)
                (chanUnlock ch me)
                (chanNotify ch)
                (set state 1)
              }
              (if (== (chanGet ch 6) 1)
                {
                  (chanUnlock ch me)
                  (set state 2)
                }
                (let ((seen (chanPark ch)))
                  {
                    (chanUnlock ch me)
                    (chanSleep ch seen)
                  }))))))
      (if (== state 1)
        (Some got)
        None)
    }))

; `chanSend`, waiting at most `nanos` nanoseconds - for the lock and for
; room. `Ok True` once `v` is in the ring; `Ok False` if the channel is
; closed; `Err` with code `sysTimedOut` when the time ran out first, and
; `Err` with code `chanOwnerDead` when the channel is poisoned - and in
; each of those `v` was not sent. The ring is looked at once more after
; the last wait, so a slot that opened as the time ran out is taken
; rather than refused. A non-positive `nanos` is one look, like
; `chanTrySend`, that says which it was.
(pub :: chanSendTimeout (-> Chan Int Int (Result Bool Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (chanSendTimeout c v nanos)
  (let (
    (ch (chanAt c))
    (me chanMe)
    (b (chanScratch nanos))
    (mut state 0)
  )
    {
      (while (== state 0)
        (let ((got (chanLock ch me b 1)))
          (if (== got 1)
            (set state 3)
            (if (== got 2)
              (set state 4)
              (let (
                (head (chanGet ch 3))
                (tail (chanGet ch 4))
                (cap (chanGet ch 5))
              )
                (if (== (chanGet ch 6) 1)
                  {
                    (chanUnlock ch me)
                    (set state 2)
                  }
                  (if (< (- tail head) cap)
                    {
                      (chanPut ch (+ 8 (% tail cap)) v)
                      (chanPut ch 4 (+ tail 1))
                      (chanBump ch)
                      (chanUnlock ch me)
                      (chanNotify ch)
                      (set state 1)
                    }
                    (if (<= (chanTimeLeft b) 0)
                      {
                        (chanUnlock ch me)
                        (set state 3)
                      }
                      (let (
                        (seen (chanPark ch))
                        (slice (chanSlice b))
                      )
                        {
                          (chanUnlock ch me)
                          (chanTimeSpent b (chanSleepFor ch seen slice) slice)
                        })))))))))
      (chanBlockDone b)
      (if (== state 3)
        (Err (mkError sysTimedOut "chanSendTimeout: timed out"))
        (if (== state 4)
          (Err (mkError chanOwnerDead "chanSendTimeout: a binding died holding the channel"))
          (Ok (== state 1))))
    }))

; `chanRecv`, waiting at most `nanos` nanoseconds - for the lock and for
; a word. `Ok (Some w)` the oldest word; `Ok None` the end of the stream
; (closed and drained); `Err` with code `sysTimedOut` when the time ran
; out first - the defined answer on timeout, which takes nothing out of
; the ring - and `Err` with code `chanOwnerDead` when the channel is
; poisoned. A non-positive `nanos` is one look.
(pub :: chanRecvTimeout (-> Chan Int (Result (Option Int) Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (chanRecvTimeout c nanos)
  (let (
    (ch (chanAt c))
    (me chanMe)
    (b (chanScratch nanos))
    (mut state 0)
    (mut got 0)
  )
    {
      (while (== state 0)
        (let ((locked (chanLock ch me b 1)))
          (if (== locked 1)
            (set state 3)
            (if (== locked 2)
              (set state 4)
              (let (
                (head (chanGet ch 3))
                (tail (chanGet ch 4))
                (cap (chanGet ch 5))
              )
                (if (< head tail)
                  {
                    (set got (chanGet ch (+ 8 (% head cap))))
                    (chanPut ch 3 (+ head 1))
                    (chanBump ch)
                    (chanUnlock ch me)
                    (chanNotify ch)
                    (set state 1)
                  }
                  (if (== (chanGet ch 6) 1)
                    {
                      (chanUnlock ch me)
                      (set state 2)
                    }
                    (if (<= (chanTimeLeft b) 0)
                      {
                        (chanUnlock ch me)
                        (set state 3)
                      }
                      (let (
                        (seen (chanPark ch))
                        (slice (chanSlice b))
                      )
                        {
                          (chanUnlock ch me)
                          (chanTimeSpent b (chanSleepFor ch seen slice) slice)
                        })))))))))
      (chanBlockDone b)
      (if (== state 1)
        (Ok (Some got))
        (if (== state 2)
          (Ok None)
          (if (== state 4)
            (Err (mkError chanOwnerDead "chanRecvTimeout: a binding died holding the channel"))
            (Err (mkError sysTimedOut "chanRecvTimeout: timed out")))))
    }))

; Send without waiting for room: `True` if `v` went into the ring,
; `False` if it did not - full, closed or poisoned, which `chanClosed`
; and `chanPoisoned` tell apart, as they do for `chanTryRecv`. A `Bool`
; rather than a three-way `Int`: a -1 for "closed" is the sentinel
; convention the error model is migrating away from
; (`tests/compat/verify-compat.py`).
(pub :: chanTrySend (-> Chan Int Bool))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (chanTrySend c v)
  (let (
    (ch (chanAt c))
    (me chanMe)
  )
    (if (!= (chanLock ch me 0 0) 0)
      false
      (let (
        (head (chanGet ch 3))
        (tail (chanGet ch 4))
        (cap (chanGet ch 5))
      )
        (if (== (chanGet ch 6) 1)
          {
            (chanUnlock ch me)
            false
          }
          (if (< (- tail head) cap)
            {
              (chanPut ch (+ 8 (% tail cap)) v)
              (chanPut ch 4 (+ tail 1))
              (chanBump ch)
              (chanUnlock ch me)
              (chanNotify ch)
              true
            }
            {
              (chanUnlock ch me)
              false
            }))))))

; Receive without waiting for a word: the oldest word, or `None` when
; there is none right now - empty, whether or not it is closed, or
; poisoned; `chanClosed` and `chanPoisoned` tell them apart.
(pub :: chanTryRecv (-> Chan (Option Int)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (chanTryRecv c)
  (let (
    (ch (chanAt c))
    (me chanMe)
  )
    (if (!= (chanLock ch me 0 0) 0)
      None
      (let (
        (head (chanGet ch 3))
        (tail (chanGet ch 4))
        (cap (chanGet ch 5))
      )
        (if (< head tail)
          (let ((v (chanGet ch (+ 8 (% head cap)))))
            {
              (chanPut ch 3 (+ head 1))
              (chanBump ch)
              (chanUnlock ch me)
              (chanNotify ch)
              (Some v)
            })
          {
            (chanUnlock ch me)
            None
          })))))

; End the stream. Idempotent. Every waiter wakes: a sender to be
; refused, a receiver to drain and then see `None`. A poisoned channel
; has ended already, and this changes nothing.
(pub :: chanClose (-> Chan Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (chanClose c)
  (let (
    (ch (chanAt c))
    (me chanMe)
  )
    (if (!= (chanLock ch me 0 0) 0)
      0
      {
        (chanPut ch 6 1)
        (chanBump ch)
        (chanUnlock ch me)
        (chanNotify ch)
        0
      })))

; Whether the stream has ended: closed, or poisoned.
(pub :: chanClosed (-> Chan Bool))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (chanClosed c)
  (let (
    (ch (chanAt c))
    (me chanMe)
  )
    (if (!= (chanLock ch me 0 0) 0)
      true
      (let ((c (chanGet ch 6)))
        {
          (chanUnlock ch me)
          (== c 1)
        }))))

; Whether a binding died holding this channel's lock, which poisoned it
; (the module header's "a holder that dies"). Takes no lock.
(pub :: chanPoisoned (-> Chan Bool))
(pub fn (chanPoisoned c)
  (chanIsPoisoned (chanAt c)))

; Words in the ring now; 0 on a poisoned channel, which yields none.
(pub :: chanLen (-> Chan Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (chanLen c)
  (let (
    (ch (chanAt c))
    (me chanMe)
  )
    (if (!= (chanLock ch me 0 0) 0)
      0
      (let ((n (- (chanGet ch 4) (chanGet ch 3))))
        {
          (chanUnlock ch me)
          n
        }))))

(pub :: chanCap (-> Chan Int))
(pub fn (chanCap c)
  (chanGet (chanAt c) 5))

; Unmap the channel. Only once no binding can still reach it - after
; the `parallel` form that used it (the module header's obligation).
; The handle is retired before the ring is unmapped, so every call made
; after this one - a second `chanFree` included - traps with status 85.
; It takes no lock, so a poisoned channel is freed like any other.
(pub :: chanFree (-> Chan (Result Int Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (chanFree c)
  (let ((ch (chanRetire c)))
    (sysUnmapShared ch (chanGet ch 7))))
